# Blend `legs` into ONE trajectory, the arm passing junction k (the end of
# leg k, the start of leg k+1) still moving where blend_radius[k] > 0.
# Planning only — nothing moves, and this planner never touches the arm.
#
# Each leg is a trajectory this planner returned (PlanToPose, PlanToJoints,
# PlanThrough, or Blend), from rest to rest, planned from where the leg before
# it ends, so each carries its own constraint (a HOLD_LEVEL leg, a free one).
# The legs are overlapped in time, as PlanThrough overlaps its own: every
# overlap keeps the joint velocity and acceleration limits and is
# collision-checked, state by state, against the current world; the whole
# trajectory is then validated like any plan.
# Each leg: joint_names in the planner's order, evenly spaced points, each with
# positions and velocities.
trajectory_msgs/JointTrajectory[] legs
# Per junction (one fewer than legs), m: how far the tool may pass from the
# planned path there; 0 stops on it.
float64[] blend_radius
# Per leg, (0, 1] of its planned speed; empty: all 1. The result carries the
# speeds, so it is executed at speed scale 1.
float64[] speed_scale
---
bool success
string message                              # failure explanation when !success
trajectory_msgs/JointTrajectory trajectory  # at the legs' speed scales
float64 planning_time                       # seconds
# Per junction: how far the tool passes from the planned path there, m (0
# where the trajectory stops).
float64[] corner_m
---
string state
